{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1564393-origin-of-aesfotedia","id":1564393,"title":"Origin of 'Aesfotedia'","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2026-09-28T12:14:16-07:00","id":1564393,"title":"Origin of 'Aesfotedia'","content":"<i>Infernalism: The Path of Screams</i> gives the name 'Aesfotedia' to the dark tass that the various infernalists of the book use, but it doesn't explains why it's called that.<br /> <br /> Like, Aesfotedia seems like a misspelling of 'Asafoetida', and I did wonder if the idea was a reference to it smelling bad? Though that still seems a bit random.<br /> <br /> Any ideas? Or have I missed something?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-09-28T13:36:19-07:00","id":1564399,"title":"","content":"This is just a guess based on the meaning of the real words within the term.<br /> <br /> &quot;Fotedia&quot; is clearly based on the Latin word foetidus which means bad smelling. I assume that is because of the traditional association of sulfur (brimstone) with the demonic and underworld as sulfur has a bad smell and comes from underground sources (hot springs and volcanic activity).<br /> <br /> &quot;Aes&quot; is probably based on the same Latin word which has multiple meanings. It means bronze or brass, and then was applied to bronze or brass coins, and thus money, but it is probably the meaning of it as &quot;base metals&quot; that is intended. The noble metals are known for not corroding. Base metals oxidize, so they corrode and therefore lose value.<br /> <br /> So if we combine the two, the term has the association of corroding substance that stinks which speaks of it as a corrupt or corrupting essence.<br /> <br /> In alchemy, of course, the idea is to turn base metals (like lead) into gold. Gold is a symbol for eternal life. So the alchemical process is actually not about ignorant chemistry, it is about spiritual purification. The idea being once you figure out how to purify a base metal into gold, you can apply the same technique into purifying the human spirit.<br /> <br /> So a term like aesfotedia is like the opposite of a pure spiritual substance. It is the exact opposite and thus symbolizes the infernalist's descent into utter damnation of the soul.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-09-28T21:50:31-07:00","id":1564405,"title":"","content":"There are some other layers.<br /> <br /> Aes became a shorthand for trade and obligations due to money.<br /> <br /> But it was also used in antiquity to reference to tonality in a poetic way. Not color, exactly, but tone. Brazen or coppery referred to something bright, but with a peculiar quality, like the sky during a sunset. Some theories hold that it was a reference to a darkened or fire-like light, still bright but definitely not white light or clearer yellow.<br /> <br /> Foto is the romance spelling of Photo, Greek for light.<br /> <br /> -edia is a suffix in Latin for collections or some objects or things with a particular relationship with a collection or corpus. Encyclopedia and media are two words that still carry this suffix.<br /> <br /> Then Aes - Foto - edia can be a repository of darkened or corrupted light. And currency. And corrosion. And so forth, it probably accepts all the above.<br /> <br /> I think the conclusion of Aes as reference to base metals a little too much, but maybe not. Even if it oxidizes, Copper and Bronze were still valuable metals, copper was indeed the third noble metal after silver, and its oxidation more deemed an ok or even desirable patina than corrosion, as it protects the metal and don't really worsen its properties in any appreciable time frame (look at Lady Liberty, she's pure copper).<br /> <br /> But then, this patina may be the reference behind ancient Aes as tonality reference. The idea that Homer was referencing the Aegean sunset is popular in the academia, but not certain.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-09-29T01:02:49-07:00","id":1564410,"title":"","content":"So are we concluding that this is a word that Brucato probably constructed specifically for <i>Infernalism</i> and only coincidently looks like the name of the cooking ingredient?<br /> <br /> In any case, I love the two etymologies. It'd fit right into the setting to have the precise meaning be kinda vague.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-09-29T01:41:54-07:00","id":1564412,"title":"","content":"I doubt it was a coincidence. The similarity to Asafoetida was probably part of the joke. Fetida/Foetida does mean &quot;stinky, foul smelling&quot;, and asafetida got this name due to a strong sulfurous smell, it's also appropriate for dark, tainted Tass used by those in communion with Hell.<br /> <br /> Working with asafetida has its challenges. If it is cooked, it smells and tastes like a mix of onions and garlic, or so I was told. But raw it is a horrific thing you have to keep sealed if you don't want your kitchen turned uninhabitable.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-09-29T04:09:12-07:00","id":1564415,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1564393-origin-of-aesfotedia?p=1564412#post1564412\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">...it's also appropriate for dark, tainted Tass used by those in communion with Hell.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sure, but lots of ingredients smell bad. Why go for this specific one? There's no wider cultural symbolism that I can find.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-09-29T12:12:42-07:00","id":1564422,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1564393-origin-of-aesfotedia?p=1564415#post1564415\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Sure, but lots of ingredients smell bad. Why go for this specific one? There's no wider cultural symbolism that I can find.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sulfur. Its smell is specifically sulfurous, the choice of words was deliberate. And I don't think you understood just how powerful the smell is.<br /> <br /> Asafetida is literally named &quot;fetid resin&quot;. It isn't just stinky, it's overpowering to an uncommon degree that made it particularly known. The heat of cooking kills it considerably, so I'm talking something that makes onions and garlic look tame in comparison.<br /> <br /> Now, I'm not saying it is the main reason for the name Aesfotedia, just that the similarity is probably intentional, not a coincidence, but the other layers are still there.<br /> <br /> But don't underestimate asafetida's capacity of specifically smelling like the Medieval Christian conception of Hell.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}